=== rozdzial_dziewiaty ===
# tlo: glodna_zima
# rozdzial: Chapter Nine
# tytul: The Wedding
The old folk had not been wrong. In October the hunger could not yet be seen, but by December it sat at every table.
On Christmas Eve not a single cottage had its twelve dishes. Bread was baked with bran and dried goosefoot, and towards the end of winter with ground birch bark as well – bitter, and so hard it made your teeth ache. Hunger had its own smell – sour, like spoiled cabbage at the bottom of a barrel – and its own sound: children crying on the other side of the wall, a weeping that did not stop even at night. A few families loaded their belongings onto sledges and left for town. None came back. # pokaz: sanie
{ ekwipunek ? monety:
    ~ ekwipunek -= monety
    ~ monety_na_make = true
}
Ice had locked the sawmill wheel, but all winter Urbaniak had men felling in the forest and paid for the work in flour. Whoever had hungry children at home went – and nobody asked any more which trees were going under the axe.{ monety_na_make: The silver Jaromir had once brought home from the sawmill and kept under his straw mattress went on flour in December.}
Jaromir fished through holes in the ice and set snares along the edge of the woods{obietnica_bor:, but he never went in among the spruces – he had once promised his {figurka_u_staruchy && los_psa == oswojony:grandmother|father} and he kept his word, even when a hare with a noose round its neck bolted from him into the thicket|. Sometimes, when a hare tore free of the snare, Jaromir followed it a few dozen paces in among the spruces – never further than the place where the birds fell silent}. {noga_ojca == zlamana:His father would sit by the window with a stick propped against his knee|His father would sit by the window with his crutches propped against the bench} mending harness, and as he worked he taught his son everything he could no longer do himself: how to sharpen a ploughshare, how to read in the sky that a thaw was coming, how to haggle with the merchants in Bukowiec so that both sides went away content. # pokaz: przerebel
{ uboze == nakarmione:
    Only in their larder did the flour somehow refuse to run out. Every week his mother sifted it, and each time she marvelled that the bottom of the bin was not yet in sight.
}
In spring, when the snow had gone from the fields, it turned out they had survived. Not everyone in Zapadlina could say as much. # pokaz: odwilz # dalej
-> piec_lat

= piec_lat
# tlo: piec_lat
In the years that followed Jaromir sowed in spring, brought the grain into the barn in autumn, and in winter earned a little extra hauling timber. His shoulders were broader than his father’s now, and in Bukowiec no one dared cheat him at the scales.
Year by year the forest crept towards the village. Nobody ever saw anyone planting trees, and yet after each thaw a new row of spruces sprang up at the margin of the fields – a few paces closer than the last. The wayside shrines below the forest were never rebuilt. # pokaz: swierki
{ kapliczki_drogi == obronil_kapliczke:
    Only one remained, at the very edge of the woods – the one Jaromir had once defended. Every morning someone laid fresh flowers beneath it, though no one in the village would own to it.
}
Antek was apprenticed in the smithy to Hanka’s father and had grown into a strong fellow – a man of few words, calm, the kind you could always count on.
Franek had left home that same hungry winter. He knocked together a shelter by the lake, beyond the reeds, and more and more often vanished into the forest for days at a time. He grew shaggy and thin and stopped coming to church. People said it was the rusalki who had changed him so, and they gave the shelter a wide berth. Only Antek went there every evening, with a loaf wrapped in a kerchief.
In the fifth summer after the famine, just after the harvest, Antek stopped Jaromir outside the smithy.
“Talk to him,” he asked, without lifting his eyes from the horseshoe. “He won’t listen to me any more. He always listened to you.”
Jaromir went down to the lake at dusk. The shelter smelled of smoke and fish, and on a plank shelf lay a row of loaves – grey, hard as stone, nibbled by mice. Franek sat barefoot by the hearth. His hair hung to his shoulders and his cheeks were so sunken that for a moment Jaromir did not know him. But when Franek raised his head, he laughed just as he used to, from ear to ear. # pokaz: franek
“Antek sent you,” he said. It was not a question – he knew.
They talked a long time about nothing much – about the sawmill, about fish, about how the village head’s Wojtek had grown fat as a hog. Not once about the rusalki. When Jaromir got up to go, Franek jerked his chin towards the shelf.
“Tell Antek to stop bringing me bread. I’m not hungry any more.” He smiled crookedly. “I can’t tell him myself. Not when he looks at me like that.”
Then he reached under his bedding and pressed into Jaromir’s hands something wrapped in a burdock leaf – a comb of wild honey, heavy and sticky, smelling of heather.
“For Hanka, to bake with. For the wedding loaves, when you finally find the nerve,” he said. “In case I don’t come.”
At the door Jaromir looked back once more. Franek was not looking at the lake but towards the dark side, where the forest began. “The water calls me every night,” he said quietly. “At least the woods don’t sing.”
Antek heard it all out and nodded – and the next evening he went down to the lake again with bread in his kerchief.
On the feast of Our Lady of the Sowing, as they were coming back from Bukowiec with blessed seed grain knotted in cloths, Hanka drew level with Jaromir on the road and for a long while walked beside him in silence.
“The village head’s Wojtek sent a matchmaker yesterday,” she said at last, not looking at him. “Father made him welcome, questioned him and sent him packing. Said he was waiting for someone else.” She shifted the knotted cloth on her shoulder. “Only that someone is taking his time. I’ll wait till All Saints’. No longer.”
Jaromir wanted to answer, but the words stuck in his throat. So he caught her hand and did not let go until the first cottages of Zapadlina, though the neighbours turned round on their carts to stare after them.
In autumn, when Jaromir had turned nineteen, his father called him out to the ledge by the wall. # pokaz: przyzba
“It’s time you were wed,” he said. “A farm without a farmwife is half a farm. And you’ve long known which cottage to send the matchmaker to.” # dalej
