# tlo: swaty
Old Staś, though bent double by now and nearly blind, insisted that he and no one else would be the matchmaker. He tied a red ribbon round a bottle of vodka, put on his Sunday sheepskin coat and waited for Jaromir by the fence from noon onwards.
# pytanie: How will Jaromir go about the matchmaking?
* [Go matchmaking the old way – with Staś and the vodka.]
    ~ swaty = po_staremu
    In the evening they stood on the threshold of the smith’s cottage. Staś took off his cap and began the way matchmakers had spoken for centuries: # pokaz: w_izbie
    “We’ve heard, master, that you’ve a young heifer in your byre for sale. We’ve come to see whether she mightn’t be just right for our young bachelor.”
    Jaromir stood behind him, crumpling his cap in sweaty hands, his ears burning as if with fever. The room smelled of fresh bread, and Hanka’s mother kept straightening her headscarf, as though it were she who was being courted.
    The smith made a show of thinking it over, his wife set glasses on the table, and Staś poured the vodka – first for the master of the house, then for himself. Hanka sat in the corner with her eyes lowered to her needlework and only once looked up at Jaromir. When the smith had drunk, the girl stood, unpinned the ribbon from her plait and tied it round the bottle – a sign that she consented. # pokaz: wstazka
    They drank to the betrothal that very evening. The smith, merry with drink, declared that if it was to be the old way, then the wedding would be the old way too – for the whole village, with a band from Bukowiec. To pay for the band and the vodka Jaromir had to sell the heifer he had been raising for two years to be his own cow.
    “We came for a heifer and gave away our own,” Staś would laugh afterwards. For a whole week he walked about the village as straight-backed as in his youth, telling everyone that this had been his last matchmaking and his best. # dalej
    -> zniknal_franek
*   [Go alone, straight to the smith.]
    ~ swaty = bez_swata
    Staś was waiting by the fence, just as he had promised. Jaromir stopped beside him only for a moment.
    “I’ll go on my own, Grandfather,” he said. “Hanka knows well enough what I’m coming for.”
    Staś made no answer. He stood a while longer with the bottle in his hand, then turned back to his cottage, dragging his feet.
    The smith was standing at the forge when Jaromir walked into the smithy with no matchmaker and no vodka. # pokaz: kuznia
    “I’ve come to ask for Hanka,” he said from the doorway.
    The smith set down his hammer and looked him over from under his bushy brows.
    “No matchmaker? No vodka? That’s not how it’s done, not the old way.” He shook his head, then burst out laughing. “You’ve pluck, I’ll grant you that. But it’s not me you should be asking – ask her.”
    “I will,” said Hanka from the threshold. She must have been standing there all along. # pokaz: hanka
    For two weeks Hanka’s mother did not say a single word to Jaromir. Her only daughter had been taken without a matchmaker, as if the groom had neither family nor neighbours, and half the village had something to gossip about at the well. And old Staś, whenever Jaromir passed him, turned his head away. # dalej
