# tlo: antek_wiesc
The priest in Bukowiec read the banns three Sundays running. The wedding was set for the first Saturday after All Saints’, when the grain was already in the barns and there was still a little time left before Advent.
A week before the wedding Antek came to the cottage, white as linen. # pokaz: antek
“Franek’s gone,” he said from the doorway. “Three days now. The bread’s not been touched. Yesterday’s, the day before’s. Hearth’s cold. His knife on the table, his cap.” He swallowed. “He’d not go even for water without his knife. Mother’s sitting on the doorstep, not crying. Grandfather’s wading about the reeds with a boat hook. It isn’t the water.” He raised his eyes to Jaromir. “Will you come up to the forest with me?”
# pytanie: What will Jaromir do?
*   [Go with Antek to the edge of the forest.]
    ~ szukanie_franka = na_skraju_boru
    # tlo: skraj_kapota
    They walked along the last row of spruces, calling by turns until they were hoarse. The woods gave no answer. On the lowest branch of the first spruce hung Franek’s coat – neatly, as if on a peg in the passage, only turned inside out, lining outwards. # pokaz: kapota
    “Inside out,” Antek said dully. “That’s what you do so the woods’ll take you for one of their own.”
    { obietnica_bor:
        Antek wanted to go further. Jaromir stayed at the boundary – he had once promised his {figurka_u_staruchy && los_psa == oswojony:grandmother|father} that he would not enter the forest – and listened to his friend’s calls drifting away among the trunks, fading and coming back. Antek came out only at nightfall, scratched, empty-handed. He spoke not one word of reproach – and that hurt more than if he had shouted.
    - else:
        They went in among the trees together. A few dozen paces on, the tits fell silent, even the rustling in the treetops died away, and both understood without a word that they must go no deeper. They backed out, as one backs out of a stranger’s room.
    }
    They searched two more days. All that while the cabbages stood in the field, and the first frost nipped them in their beds. His mother said nothing, only turned the frozen, glassy heads over and over in her hands. For the winter there was half a barrel of sauerkraut. # dalej
    -> kuznia_przed_slubem
* [Not put off the wedding – let Antek search on his own.]
    ~ szukanie_franka = antek_sam
    “The wedding’s in a week,” said Jaromir. “I can’t, not now…”
    For a long time Antek said nothing.
    “You’re getting married,” he said quietly. “And my brother’s out there somewhere in those woods, and nobody but me is looking for him.”
    He turned and walked off without waiting for an answer. # pokaz: odchodzi
    He came back two days later with Franek’s coat under his arm. All he said was that it had been hanging on the lowest branch of the first spruce, neatly, as if on a peg in the passage – turned inside out, lining outwards. After that he did not speak a word to Jaromir until the wedding. Meanwhile Jaromir brought the cabbages in from the field, a day before the first frost – and never before had he been so ashamed of a job well done. # dalej
    -> kuznia_przed_slubem
*   [Go to Hanka’s grandmother for advice.]
    ~ szukanie_franka = rada_babki
    # tlo: izba_babki
    The old woman heard him out in silence, turning dried herbs between her fingers. # pokaz: babka
    “Whom the forest calls, you’ll not hold back,” she said at last. “I’m telling you: don’t go looking for him among the trees now, or you’ll be lost yourself.” She got up and closed the shutter on the forest side, though it was broad daylight. “Pray for him. And don’t go out to the edge of the woods after dark, not even if he calls you by name.” # pokaz: okno
    { glos_w_lesie:
        Jaromir remembered his mother’s voice {figurka_u_staruchy:out of the mist over the field|from beyond the first spruces} on that Christmas Eve night, and did not try to argue.
    }
    Hanka, who had been spinning by the stove, put down her distaff.
    “It’s right you won’t let him go, Grandmother,” she said. “But Antek isn’t going to search on his own. I’ll go with him along the water, where old Staś is looking. Nobody’s calling me.”
    Her grandmother pressed her lips together but did not stop her. Hanka came back after midnight, soaked to the knees, empty-handed. All Antek brought back was his brother’s coat – it had been hanging on the lowest branch of the first spruce, turned inside out, lining outwards. In the morning Hanka woke hoarse, and right up to the wedding she could speak only in a whisper. # dalej
    -> kuznia_przed_slubem

