# tlo: kuznia_wieczor
On the eve of the wedding the smith called Jaromir to the smithy. Inside it smelled of iron and charcoal, the bellows wheezed like a tired horse, and the embers in the forge pulsed red with every breath they drew.
{ licho_noc == odpedzone:
    He had rebuilt the smithy himself, beam by beam, after the fire that lean summer, but his hands had been burned for good – the skin on them was shiny and tight, and he held his hammer now as if it weighed twice what it used to.
}
On the anvil lay two things: a horseshoe, still warm, and a small iron cross on a leather thong. # pokaz: kowadlo
“Iron from the forge keeps off what comes by night,” said the smith. “So my grandfather used to say, and his grandfather before him. I can’t give Hanka the dowry she’d wish for{licho_noc == odpedzone:, for all I had went into rebuilding the smithy|, for a village smith never grows rich}. But this I can.” He pointed at the anvil. “One of the two. Choose. The horseshoe over the threshold – so nothing evil comes into your cottage. Or the cross for Hanka – so nothing evil touches her, wherever she goes.”
# pytanie: What will Jaromir choose?
*   [Take the horseshoe.]
    ~ ekwipunek += podkowa
    Jaromir took the horseshoe. It was heavier than he had expected. # pokaz: podkowa
    “Nail it up with the ends pointing skywards,” the smith told him. “So the luck doesn’t spill out.” # dalej
    -> slub
*   [Take the cross for Hanka.]
    ~ ekwipunek += krzyzyk
    Jaromir took the cross and weighed it in his palm. It was tiny, but cold as ice, though a moment before it had lain on the hot anvil. # pokaz: krzyzyk
    “Tell her not to take it off,” the smith said quietly. “Ever.” # dalej
